#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111660} :activity/id #uuid "a679a0bc-f291-575b-bac5-97356c792140" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049799 :topic/id #uuid "14d86bef-ffa1-58b3-9249-a9e29d8bde2a" :topic/math-academy-id 1540} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111660 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111894 :tutorial/content "The graph of the square root function\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4d6f457f29cc6a292f23029967dc6fbb0c0f3905a04f34a430fddc8b211b0c3.png)\n\nIn this lesson, we will learn how to apply graph transformations to the square root function. Let's start by considering translations." :tutorial/id #uuid "ff943ba5-7959-599a-8f13-e5bdb1bda83a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4775 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2d1bbdf9-422b-5f69-8f59-21d910778f63" :step/math-academy-id 464534 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111895 :tutorial/content "We can move the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nup or down by adding or subtracting a number. For example, to obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}-3\n$$\n\nwe perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Translate the graph **down** by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}-3$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/1b/1b5eadb281202323377386e6b750a304ece8f39232d0c37dc5d4885004a6b588.png)\n\nSimilarly, we can also move the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x}\n$$\n\nto the left or to the right by adding or subtracting a number to the variable $x$ under the square root. For instance, to obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{x-3}\n$$\n\nwe perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Translate the graph to the **right** by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x-3}$.\n\nThe result is the solid blue curve shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d51b569648468c78445daa6b8dc658fe6e2e8cb24ca8308b60a1f40b07c7e29.png)\n\nRemember:\n\n- Shifting a function up/down/left/right does *not* change the shape of a graph.\n- If $a$ is a positive number, then a transformation of the type $y=f(x\\pm a)$ is *counter-intuitive*: $f(x+a)$ moves the graph to the *left* by $a$ units, while $f(x-a)$ moves the graph to the *right* by $a$ units." :tutorial/id #uuid "7bdd8c52-b035-531e-b5ba-085731e44771" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4776 :tutorial/title "Vertical and Horizontal Translations"} :step/id #uuid "91171f12-b0b8-5ca8-afcf-bde7eb9fb6fe" :step/math-academy-id 464535 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111662}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111662 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110708 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110531 :question/id #uuid "04070131-1543-5516-a7f1-509ea3f01234" :question/math-academy-id "e-7937"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a70b1e82-4650-5de9-ad47-7578481b802a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050753}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111013}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Root Function Containing One Translation"} :step/id #uuid "66c173b6-94ff-5b36-988c-72a5f4e6623b" :step/math-academy-id 464536 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111663}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110709 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110532 :question/id #uuid "0e964fdb-fa9e-516a-badf-2217a7937bc8" :question/math-academy-id "e-7938"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3425852-1b32-552e-baaf-bf53644e53b8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111164}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Square Root Function Containing Two Translations"} :step/id #uuid "fd64edda-d116-5987-a423-30af039a0c48" :step/math-academy-id 464539 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111664}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111896 :tutorial/content "We can reflect the graph of the square root function\n\n$$\ny = \\sqrt x\n$$\n\nin both the $x$ and $y$-axis.\n\nMultiplying the $\\sqrt x$ by $-1$ reflects the curve in the $x$-axis. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt x\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/ee/eef724b522e133a2a537d1b6e1406bdbf17fe285ea43c278888ab4a4bc9fa58c.png)\n\nMultiplying the variable $x$ by $-1$ reflects the curve in the $y$-axis. The graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{-x}\n$$\n\nis shown below.\n\n![](images/78/782eade24c5ab9e1af679f0a310fac2d3e20805d40ee97c695ea214ac71cf2f0.png)\n\nNote that\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{-x}\n$$\n\nis only defined for the negative numbers and zero.\n\nNow, suppose we want to sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny= -\\sqrt{x-2}+3\n$$\n\nTo obtain the graph of\n\n$$\ny=-\\sqrt{x-2}\n$$\n\nwe can perform the following transformations:\n\n- Take the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}$.\n- Reflect the graph in the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x}$.\n\n![](images/b9/b95995b8ee5595d774f02d2c03c1170cc443b8f06f6ccbc634eeeb2aebe86357.png)\n\n- Translate the result to the right by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x-2}$.\n- Translate the result up by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\sqrt{x-2}+3$.\n\n![](images/26/263ea3a28314da17ba084bcb512976deb73483dfaa3ad149845c35634ae1d4a4.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "55172b36-dabd-52b3-8195-cbe97b913cc0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4777 :tutorial/title "Reflections"} :step/id #uuid "aa82b019-f24d-5baa-8ff4-93fdb90e7024" :step/math-academy-id 464542 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111665}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110710 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110533 :question/id #uuid "4ffc1494-2efd-581c-a58e-d1e9a8a2dfc4" :question/math-academy-id "e-7939"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adbab69f-d3db-5162-9c21-0402b95e0c74" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048753}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111163}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Reflected Square Root Function Containing One Translation"} :step/id #uuid "7124301f-084e-5b5d-b8c2-4b15e1bdd17e" :step/math-academy-id 464543 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111666}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110711 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110534 :question/id #uuid "bc4d0d1e-4cf6-5e3a-99b8-3613593fd0d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-7940"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "396377cb-7e23-5c95-9f9f-a33c210bc670" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111162}] :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Reflected Square Root Function Containing Two Translations"} :step/id #uuid "dd82d42a-f2ba-5fd4-9caa-4add435c4441" :step/math-academy-id 464546}] :activity/title "Graph Transformations of Square Root Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110880}]}
